 ## Background
-CAG-GS was proposed to address the computational and storage burdens of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3D-GS) when applied to large-scale scenes [S1]. It builds on the implicit scene representation approach where anchors predict Gaussian attributes [S1]. The method was introduced to overcome the limitations of divide-and-conquer strategies that often lead to limited performance due to inconsistent lighting and floaters [S1]. It draws inspiration from OctreeGS for hierarchical representation and integrates semantic guidance from SAM2 to improve feature learning [S1].
+CAG-GS was proposed to address the computational and storage burdens of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3D-GS) when applied to large-scale scenes [S1]. It builds on the implicit scene representation approach where anchors predict Gaussian attributes [S1]. The method was introduced to overcome the limitations of divide-and-conquer strategies that often lead to limited performance due to inconsistent lighting and floaters [S1]. The hierarchical representation follows OctreeGS [S1]. The method integrates semantic guidance from SAM2 to improve feature learning [S1].
 
